IMPORT prelude

-- National Registration Act 1965 (Singapore) -- offences, penalties, enforcement powers, composition and service:
-- ss 16 to 28 and 32.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage
-- table, no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `National Registration Act 1965 -- offences and powers`

§§ `16 -- the offences`

-- s 16(1), each "shall be liable on conviction to a fine not exceeding $5,000 or to imprisonment for a term not
-- exceeding 5 YEARS or to both":
--   (a) failing to register or re-register within the prescribed time;
--   (b) failing to report a change of residence as required under section 10;
--   (c) giving a contact address that is false or not one described in s 10(2);
--   (d) failing to submit householder returns, or to submit them in time;
--   (e) "without lawful authority or reasonable excuse, is in possession of one or more identity cards";
--   (f) unlawfully depriving any person of an identity card;
--   (g) failing or refusing to surrender a card after cancellation under s 13(2);
--   (h) defacing, mutilating or destroying an identity card.
-- s 16(2), each $10,000 or 10 YEARS or both:
--   (a) "without lawful authority, parts with possession of any identity card to any person";
--   (b) "without lawful authority or reasonable excuse, knowingly obtains or is in possession of or makes use of a forged
--       identity card or an identity card other than his or her own";
--   (c) unlawfully issuing or reproducing an identity card or any part of one;
--   (d) forging, erasing, altering or falsifying an entry, or tampering with a card.
-- s 16(3): aiding or abetting any offence -- no penalty stated in the Act.
-- s 16(4): every offence is arrestable.
--
-- Three things in this list.
--
-- (i) A missed change-of-address report, (1)(b), carries the same five years as destroying a card.
--
-- (ii) (2)(a) has "without lawful authority" but NOT "or reasonable excuse". (1)(e) and (2)(b) have both. So handing
-- your own card to anyone -- a condominium guard, a hotel receptionist, a mobile phone shop -- is a ten-year offence
-- unless some law authorises it; a reasonable excuse is no answer. s 33(2)(f) lets regulations provide for inspection of
-- cards by hotels and other prescribed persons, which may be the lawful authority; no regulations were retrieved.
--
-- (iii) (1)(e) catches possession of "one or more identity cards" without lawful authority or reasonable excuse, and
-- does not except the person's OWN card. The holder's own card is presumably held with lawful authority (s 9 issues it
-- to them), and that is taken here as the answer.
DECLARE Offence IS ONE OF
    `failing to register or re-register in time`
    `failing to report a change of residence`
    `giving a false or unpermitted contact address`
    `failing to submit householder returns`
    `possessing identity cards without lawful authority or reasonable excuse`
    `unlawfully depriving a person of an identity card`
    `failing to surrender a cancelled card`
    `defacing, mutilating or destroying an identity card`
    `parting with possession of an identity card without lawful authority`
    `knowingly using a forged card or another person's card`
    `unlawfully issuing or reproducing a card`
    `forging or tampering with a card`
    `failing to surrender a suspicious card on demand`
    `disclosing HDB information unlawfully`

GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum fine` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `parting with possession of an identity card without lawful authority` THEN 10000
    WHEN `knowingly using a forged card or another person's card` THEN 10000
    WHEN `unlawfully issuing or reproducing a card` THEN 10000
    WHEN `forging or tampering with a card` THEN 10000
    WHEN `failing to surrender a suspicious card on demand` THEN 5000
    WHEN `disclosing HDB information unlawfully` THEN 2000
    OTHERWISE 5000

-- In months.
GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum imprisonment` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `parting with possession of an identity card without lawful authority` THEN 120
    WHEN `knowingly using a forged card or another person's card` THEN 120
    WHEN `unlawfully issuing or reproducing a card` THEN 120
    WHEN `forging or tampering with a card` THEN 120
    WHEN `failing to surrender a suspicious card on demand` THEN 6
    WHEN `disclosing HDB information unlawfully` THEN 12
    OTHERWISE 60

GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a reasonable excuse is a defence` IF
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `possessing identity cards without lawful authority or reasonable excuse` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `knowingly using a forged card or another person's card` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the offence is arrestable` IF
    TRUE

-- Handing over your card.
DECLARE Handover HAS
    `parted with possession of an identity card` IS A BOOLEAN
    `some law authorised the handover` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the person had a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the handover` IS A Handover
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the handover is an offence under section 16(2)(a)` IF
        `the handover`'s `parted with possession of an identity card`
    AND NOT `the handover`'s `some law authorised the handover`

-- s 21(3) and s 16(1)(g) compared: refusing to surrender a SUSPICIOUS card on a demand is $5,000 or six months;
-- failing to surrender a card whose registration has been CANCELLED is $5,000 or five years. The second requires no
-- demand at all -- s 13(2) says "forthwith".

§§ `19 -- attempts and preparation`

-- s 19(1): "any person who attempts to commit OR DOES ANY ACT PREPARATORY to the commission of an offence under this Act
-- shall be deemed to be guilty of the offence". A preparatory act carries the full penalty of the completed offence.
-- s 19(2): knowingly assisting an offender to avoid apprehension, trial or punishment is prohibited (no penalty stated
-- in the Act).
DECLARE Stage IS ONE OF
    `the completed offence`
    `an attempt`
    `an act preparatory to the offence`
    `a mere intention with no act`

GIVEN `the stage` IS A Stage
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person is guilty of the full offence` IF
    CONSIDER `the stage`
    WHEN `a mere intention with no act` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `21 -- surrender of suspicious cards`

-- s 21(1): a registration officer or police officer may demand surrender of a card (including a document purporting to
-- be one) if there are reasonable grounds to suspect it was (a) obtained by false or misleading information, (b) used in
-- an offence under the Act, or (c) is a false identity card.
DECLARE Suspicion HAS
    `reasonable grounds: obtained by false information` IS A BOOLEAN
    `reasonable grounds: used in an offence under the Act` IS A BOOLEAN
    `reasonable grounds: a false identity card` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the suspicion` IS A Suspicion
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the officer may demand surrender` IF
        `the suspicion`'s `reasonable grounds: obtained by false information`
    OR  `the suspicion`'s `reasonable grounds: used in an offence under the Act`
    OR  `the suspicion`'s `reasonable grounds: a false identity card`

§§ `22 -- arrest without warrant`

-- s 22(1): a person may be arrested without warrant if "(a) [he or she] is reasonably suspected ... of the commission of
-- any offence under this Act; or (b) on demand by a registration officer or a police officer -- (i) does not give his
-- or her name and address; (ii) gives a name or address which the officer has reason to believe is false; or (iii)
-- GIVES AS HIS OR HER ADDRESS A PLACE OUTSIDE SINGAPORE".
--
-- Limb (iii) does not ask whether the address is TRUE. A Singaporean who lives abroad and answers a demand with their
-- true foreign address may be arrested without warrant. s 10(2) lets such a person register a Singapore contact
-- address, but limb (iii) turns on the address the person GIVES on demand.
DECLARE ArrestFacts HAS
    `reasonably suspected of an offence under the Act` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a demand for name and address was made` IS A BOOLEAN
    `gave no name and address` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the officer has reason to believe the name or address is false` IS A BOOLEAN
    `gave an address outside Singapore` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the facts` IS AN ArrestFacts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person may be arrested without warrant` IF
        `the facts`'s `reasonably suspected of an offence under the Act`
    OR  (    `the facts`'s `a demand for name and address was made`
         AND (    `the facts`'s `gave no name and address`
              OR  `the facts`'s `the officer has reason to believe the name or address is false`
              OR  `the facts`'s `gave an address outside Singapore`))

-- s 22(5): "A woman must not be searched under this section except by a woman."
GIVEN `the person searched is a woman` IS A BOOLEAN, `the searcher is a woman` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the search of the person is permitted by section 22(5)` IF
    (NOT `the person searched is a woman`) OR `the searcher is a woman`

§§ `26 -- officers must identify themselves`

-- s 26(1): when exercising a power, an officer must "(a) if not in uniform, declare his or her office; and (b) on demand
-- by any person affected ..., produce" the identification the Commissioner (or Commissioner of Police) directs.
-- s 26(2): "Despite any provision of this Act, it is NOT AN OFFENCE for a person to refuse to comply with any request,
-- demand or order made or given by a registration officer or a police officer who does not comply with subsection (1)."
DECLARE OfficerConduct HAS
    `in uniform` IS A BOOLEAN
    `declared office` IS A BOOLEAN
    `identification was demanded` IS A BOOLEAN
    `produced identification` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the officer` IS AN OfficerConduct
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the officer complied with section 26(1)` IF
        (`the officer`'s `in uniform` OR `the officer`'s `declared office`)
    AND ((NOT `the officer`'s `identification was demanded`) OR `the officer`'s `produced identification`)

GIVEN `the officer` IS AN OfficerConduct
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `refusing the officer's demand can be an offence` IF
    `the officer complied with section 26(1)` `the officer`

§§ `17 -- HDB information`

-- s 17(1), (2): a registration officer may by written notice require the Housing and Development Board to disclose
-- secret or confidential information "despite any obligation as to secrecy". s 17(3): the officer may pass it on only
-- (a) to another law enforcement officer (police, immigration or registration officer) for official duties, PROVIDED THE
-- BOARD CONSENTS; or (b) for criminal proceedings. s 17(4): $2,000 or 12 months.
DECLARE Onward HAS
    `to another law enforcement officer for official duties` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the Board consented` IS A BOOLEAN
    `for criminal proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the onward disclosure` IS AN Onward
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the onward disclosure is lawful` IF
        (`the onward disclosure`'s `to another law enforcement officer for official duties` AND `the onward disclosure`'s `the Board consented`)
    OR  `the onward disclosure`'s `for criminal proceedings`

§§ `25 -- notices to attend court`

-- s 25(5): if the alleged offender fails to appear, the court may (if satisfied of service) issue a warrant UNLESS before
-- that date "(a) in the case of an offence which may be compounded, the alleged offender has been permitted to compound
-- the offence; or (b) the notice has been cancelled".
DECLARE NoticeFacts HAS
    `failed to appear in person or by counsel` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the court is satisfied the notice was duly served` IS A BOOLEAN
    `permitted to compound before the date` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the notice was cancelled before the date` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the notice` IS A NoticeFacts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court may issue an arrest warrant` IF
        `the notice`'s `failed to appear in person or by counsel`
    AND `the notice`'s `the court is satisfied the notice was duly served`
    AND NOT `the notice`'s `permitted to compound before the date`
    AND NOT `the notice`'s `the notice was cancelled before the date`

§§ `27 -- Malaysian reports`

-- s 27: a report by a Malaysian identity-card officer or a Malaysian police fingerprint officer may be used as evidence
-- unless the court requires the person to be called; but the Public Prosecutor must deliver a copy to the accused "not
-- less than 10 clear days before the commencement of the trial".
GIVEN `clear days before trial the copy was delivered` IS A NUMBER, `the court requires the maker to be called` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Malaysian report may be used without calling its maker` IF
        `clear days before trial the copy was delivered` AT LEAST 10
    AND NOT `the court requires the maker to be called`

§§ `28 -- composition`

-- s 28(1): a prescribed compoundable offence may be compounded for "a sum not exceeding $500" -- whatever the offence's
-- maximum fine.
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the most that can be collected on composition` MEANS 500

§§ `32 -- service`

-- s 32(2): service takes effect "(a) if ... by fax and a notification of successful transmission is received, on the
-- day of transmission; (b) if ... by email, at the time that the email becomes capable of being retrieved ...; and
-- (c) if ... by prepaid registered post, 2 days after the day the document was posted (even if it is returned
-- undelivered)." s 32(3): not for court documents.
-- s 32(1)(d): affixing a copy in a conspicuous place at the residential or business address is good service; s 32(2)
-- states no time for it.
DECLARE ServiceMethod IS ONE OF
    `personal delivery`
    `registered post`
    `leaving with an adult at the address`
    `affixing at the address`
    `fax with a transmission notification`
    `email`

-- Day service takes effect, counted from the day of sending as day 0. NOTHING where s 32(2) states no time: personal
-- delivery, leaving with an adult, affixing, and email (which takes effect at a moment, not on a day count).
GIVEN `the method` IS A ServiceMethod
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`days until service takes effect` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the method`
    WHEN `registered post` THEN JUST 2
    WHEN `fax with a transmission notification` THEN JUST 0
    OTHERWISE NOTHING
